-- lego-tribute-to-leonardo-da-vinci-40902-worth-19600
UPDATE public.reviews SET content = $boi_t$Your wallet just got a reality check – a gift‑with‑purchase that hides behind a US$150 spend. A tribute to Leonardo da Vinci sounds classy, but the spend it takes to get one feels more like a down‑payment on a small car.

The [40902](/sets/40902-tribute-to-leonardo-da-vinci) Tribute to Leonardo da Vinci arrives as a Gift with Purchase (GwP), meaning you only get it when you spend a certain amount on LEGO’s online store. It isn’t a standalone launch you can snag from a local retailer, and that already puts it in a niche corner of the market. The set itself is modest: 251 pieces, a small bust of the master, a tiny easel, and a few iconic inventions like the flying machine and the Vitruvian Man. Build time hovers around an hour for most builders, which is perfect for a quick weekend session.

From a construction standpoint, the set uses a lot of classic bricks and plates, with a few specialty elements for the inventions. The techniques are straightforward – a lot of brick‑on‑brick stacking, a few snot (stud‑not‑on‑top) tricks for the flying machine’s wings, and a modest amount of internal support. If you enjoy the calm of a low‑piece count build, this will feel satisfying. If you crave the drama of large‑scale builds with intricate mechanisms, you’ll probably yawn halfway through.

Design-wise, LEGO does a decent job of capturing Leonardo’s silhouette. The bust is recognisable, and the easel adds a nice visual anchor. The included inventions are more symbolic than functional; the flying machine’s wings don’t actually move, and the Vitruvian Man is a static figure. For a collector who loves the Renaissance theme, the set hits the sweet spot. For a fan who expects moving parts or a more elaborate diorama, the set feels a bit under‑delivered.

Value is hard to judge the usual way: a gift with purchase has no price of its own, so there's no per-piece cost to compare. The specialty elements are few, so the spend it takes to unlock it isn't justified by an abundance of unique parts. Here, the appeal is purely the brand‑name tribute and the exclusivity of a GwP.

Community reaction is mixed. Some LEGO fans appreciate the homage to a historical figure and see the set as a nice addition to a Renaissance‑themed collection. Others criticize the high threshold and the lack of a functional build, calling it a “collector’s token” rather than a true building experience. The double loyalty points promotion for Insiders adds a tiny incentive, but it doesn’t offset the core issue: you have to spend a lot to get a modest set.

If you’re already planning a big cart on LEGO’s website, grabbing this set could be a decent side‑kick – especially if you love Leonardo’s legacy and enjoy displaying mini‑figures on a shelf. But for most builders, the price‑to‑experience ratio leans toward “skip it and buy a regular set.”

40902 is a gift with purchase, so LEGO doesn't sell it separately. MyBrickHouse has listed it on its own at ₹2,499, but it was out of stock when we last saw the listing on 22 Sep 2026.

On that bombshell, it's time to say goodbye. I'll see you on the next one. Bubyee.

Corrected 29 Sep 2026: an earlier version called 40902 "a $150 gift‑with‑purchase" (US$150 is the spend needed to get it), gave it 1,200 pieces (the catalogue has 251), worked out a per-piece price from those figures, and estimated an import price of ₹19,600, which also appeared in the headline. The figures are corrected or removed; MyBrickHouse's last listing was ₹2,499 (out of stock, 22 Sep 2026).

Verdict: IMPORT ONLY. Not in Indian stores — grey market or wait.$boi_t$, title = $boi_t$LEGO Tribute to Leonardo da Vinci (40902): Worth Chasing?$boi_t$, seo_title = $boi_t$LEGO Tribute to Leonardo da Vinci (40902): Worth Chasing?$boi_t$, excerpt = $boi_t$Your wallet just got a reality check – a gift‑with‑purchase that hides behind a US$150 spend. A tribute to Leonardo da Vinci sounds classy, but the spend…$boi_t$, seo_description = $boi_t$Your wallet just got a reality check – a gift‑with‑purchase that hides behind a US$150 spend. A tribute to Leonardo da Vinci sounds classy, but the spend…$boi_t$, updated_at = now()
